<article class="env-proposition" id="05K6">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/05K6">Proposition <span data-tag="05K6">7.6.2.19</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category and let </p>
<div class="equation" id="05K7">
<span class="equation-label"><a data-tag="05K7" href="/tag/05K7">7.73</a></span>
<div>\begin{equation}  \begin{gathered}\label{equation:pullback-over-discrete} \xymatrix@R =50pt@C=50pt{ X_{01} \ar [r] \ar [d] &amp;  X_0 \ar [d] \\ X_1 \ar [r] &amp;  X } \end{gathered} \end{equation}</div>
</div>
<p> be a pullback diagram in $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. If the object $X$ is discrete, then (<a data-tag="05K7" href="/tag/05K7">7.73</a>) determines a pullback diagram in the homotopy category $\operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof of Proposition <a data-tag="05K6" href="/tag/05K6">7.6.2.19</a>.</strong>
       By virtue of Proposition <a data-tag="03FU" href="/tag/03FU">7.6.2.16</a>, it will suffice to show that the tautological map $F: \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{/X} \rightarrow \operatorname{N}_{\bullet }(\operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}})_{/X}$ preserves products. Since $X$ is discrete, we can use Example <a data-tag="06RX" href="/tag/06RX">4.7.9.24</a> to identify $F$ with the canonical map from $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{/X}$ to (the nerve of) its homotopy category, which always preserves products (see Warning <a data-tag="03EL" href="/tag/03EL">7.6.1.14</a>). 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
